meta {
  name: 06 - Page Size Over Limit
  type: graphql
  seq: 6
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}
  body: graphql
  auth: inherit
}

body:graphql {
  query PageSizeOverLimit {
    issues(first: 251) {
      nodes { id }
      pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
    }
  }
}

docs {
  Reference: https://linear.app/developers/pagination

  The documented-limit +1 probe. Linear's pagination docs state the default page size
  ("The first 50 results are returned by default without query arguments") but never state
  a MAXIMUM, so the ceiling and its enforcement mode had to be established live.

  **Limitation / observed finding — max `first` is 250, and over-limit is REJECTED, not
  clamped.** Confirmed 2026-07-30. `first: 250` succeeds; `first: 251` fails with:

      HTTP 200
      { "data": null,
        "errors": [{ "message": "Argument Validation Error",
                     "path": ["issues"],
                     "extensions": {
                       "code": "INVALID_INPUT",
                       "validationErrors": [{ "property": "first", "value": 251,
                         "constraints": { "max": "first must not be greater than 250" } }],
                       "userError": true,
                       "userPresentableMessage": "first must not be greater than 250." } }] }

  Three things worth pulling out:

  - **Rejected, not clamped, and not truncated.** `data` is null and NO page is returned.
    There is no silent-data-loss mode here; the connector must clamp `first` to <= 250
    itself before sending. A too-large page size fails the whole sync loudly.
  - **HTTP 200 on a hard failure.** This is the cleanest demonstration in the collection
    that Linear's status code carries no success signal. A connector that checks only
    `response.status` would read `data: null` as an empty page and silently conclude the
    stream is exhausted. Every response must be checked for a top-level `errors` array.
  - The ceiling is an ARGUMENT VALIDATION constraint, so unlike the complexity numbers in
    `05 - Complexity Envelope` it is independent of how much data the workspace holds.
    250 is a real, portable maximum.

  The same 250 ceiling applies to `projects`, `initiatives`, and `issueLabels` — all four
  root fields take `first: Int` against the same validator (see
  `02 - Introspect Pagination Args`).
}

example {
  name: 200 + errors — first=251 rejected, not clamped (data: null)

  request: {
    url: https://api.linear.app/graphql
    method: POST
    mode: graphql

    body:graphql: {
      query PageSizeOverLimit {
        issues(first: 251) {
          nodes { id }
          pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  response: {
    headers: {
      x-complexity: 3
      x-ratelimit-requests-limit: 2500
      x-ratelimit-requests-remaining: 2494
      x-ratelimit-complexity-limit: 3000000
      x-ratelimit-complexity-remaining: 2999991
    }

    status: {
      code: 200
      text: OK
    }

    body: {
      type: json
      content: '''
        {
          "errors": [
            {
              "message": "Argument Validation Error",
              "path": [
                "issues"
              ],
              "locations": [
                {
                  "line": 2,
                  "column": 3
                }
              ],
              "extensions": {
                "code": "INVALID_INPUT",
                "validationErrors": [
                  {
                    "target": {
                      "first": 251
                    },
                    "value": 251,
                    "property": "first",
                    "children": [],
                    "constraints": {
                      "max": "first must not be greater than 250"
                    }
                  }
                ],
                "type": "invalid input",
                "userError": true,
                "userPresentableMessage": "first must not be greater than 250."
              }
            }
          ],
          "data": null
        }
      '''
    }
  }
}
